00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.500
In 2008, China hosted its first ever Olympic
Games.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.790
Over 10,000 athletes, from 200 countries,
competed in 300 summer events.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:22.560
But for China, it was about much more than
athletics,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.990
In many ways, this was Beijing’s grand entrance
to the world.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.850
As the most-watched televised event in history,
at the time,

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:37.160
it was the perfect opportunity to show off
a healthy, happy, prosperous China to an international

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.329
audience,

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:43.800
one that has long been confused about and
often deeply suspicious, of the Middle Kingdom.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.010
So, its government spared no expense.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.820
The city was given an extreme makeover.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.630
The kind you can afford when your economy
relies on pouring concrete on any surface

00:00:54.630 --> 00:01:01.420
you can find, and then pouring again because
why not?, more labor means more economic growth.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.260
9 Billion dollars was spent improving public
transit, doubling the size of the subway.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:14.460
Ugly powerlines were buried underground, flowers
planted, and twenty new buildings constructed,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:20.060
like the iconic Bird’s Nest, Which held
the Opening Ceremony on August 8th, 2008,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.229
at exactly 8:08 PM, a lucky number in China.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:28.130
The 4-hour event cost 100 million dollars.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:29.560
7,000 per.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.560
second.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.799
And flying overhead, you would’ve noticed
that, although it was raining elsewhere that

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:39.109
night, the sky above the open-roofed stadium
was perfectly clear.

00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:43.899
Only minutes after the ceremony ended, the
clouds magically reappeared.

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:50.240
The event was so important, and China so determined,
that it changed the weather, literally shooting

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.450
chemicals at the sky with rocket launchers.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:59.999
And yet, even when its image mattered the
most, China still couldn’t control its pollution.

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:04.880
The city was covered in its signature, dangerously
thick, grey smog.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.959
Air quality was so bad that some athletes
changed events.

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:11.650
Others decided not to compete at all.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:17.430
But what looks like a hopeless environmental
disaster, China sees as an amazing economic

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.819
opportunity.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:24.920
Its now on a quest to clear its air, clean
its energy, and grow its economy, not despite

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:30.090
these things, but because of them.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.269
There are two ways to look at environmental
impact, depending on whose payroll you’re

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:36.269
on.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:42.319
Per capita, China’s CO2 emissions, for example,
are nothing special, About the same as Poland

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.160
or Mongolia.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.120
Nowhere near a rich country like the U.S.,
the United Arab Emirates, or especially, Qatar.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.120
Wow.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.830
But, in total, China makes up a quarter of
the world’s emissions.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.579
With a population of 1.3 billion, it’s problem
is that it’s just so.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:01.870
darn. big.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:07.299
As the world’s largest car market, China
has as many motor vehicles as the U.S. has

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:10.409
people, Three hundred twenty-two million.

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:17.299
So, not only does it have that fun LA traffic,
but also the kind of pollution that stops

00:03:17.299 --> 00:03:18.750
planes from landing,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.220
the kind of pollution where you can’t see
your fingers,

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.579
the kind you can vacuum up, condense, and
make a brick out of.

00:03:26.579 --> 00:03:32.379
But unlike America, where the automobile means
freedom, and freedom, well that’s our thing,

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:39.209
The right to commute in a 16 seat SUV by yourself
is probably written in the constitution, China

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:42.890
can and does just say “No.”

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:48.810
Cities like Beijing, Guangzhou, and Guiyang,
only issue a tiny number of new license plates

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.800
a year, through a lottery.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.340
Although, ”tiny” is kind of an understatement.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:01.879
The chance of winning is approximately 1 in
783, or was, until Beijing lowered the quota

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:08.390
from 90,000 to 40, and now your chances are
1 in 2,031.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.420
Locals joke they’re more likely to win the
actual lottery.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:19.220
One government official was arrested for accepting
bribes of 30,000 US Dollars for a single plate.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.660
Shanghai, on the other hand, auctions them
off to the highest bidder, with an average

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:31.700
price of $14,022, nearly twice China’s per
capita GDP, and considerably more than most

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.590
of the cars they’re attached to.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:39.510
So, good luck buying a car unless you have
very deep pockets, powerful friends, or amazing

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:40.850
luck.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.380
Even so, there are limitations.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.730
In Beijing, the last number of your license
plate determines which days you can drive

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:48.730
on.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:55.690
For example, right now, until April 7th, numbers
1 and 6 can’t drive on Monday, 2 and 7 can’t

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:59.600
on Tuesday, 3 and 8 on Wednesday, and so on.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.570
All are allowed on the weekend.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:06.720
The numbers switch four times a year, and
rule breakers get three points on their license,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.190
12 of which suspends your ability to drive.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.780
These restrictions have helped reduce pollution,
but only so much.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:20.510
The Air Quality Index, which measures pollution,
is usually in the range of 50-100 in cities

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:24.620
like LA, San Diego, and most of southern China.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.160
In the North, it’s often three, four, even
five times that much.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:35.530
Now, it’s easy to see these numbers, think
that China focuses only on economic growth

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:41.020
and conclude that its government really doesn’t
care that much about pollution.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.420
But that’s not entirely true.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.190
For one, healthcare is really expensive.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:52.160
Pollution kills an estimated 1.6 million people
in the country a year.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.710
It also has a significant impact on tourism.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:00.830
What makes this issue so unique is that it
can’t be hidden - smog is there for everyone

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:07.100
to see, and not in some far, Western province,
but in the capital, where politicians live

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.160
and work.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.480
So, even Chinese-owned state media reports
on the problem.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.410
And while it isn’t exactly known as the
protest capital of the world, it has seen

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.450
a few.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:22.610
If that wasn’t incentive enough, enter electric
cars.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.530
Perhaps nowhere on earth is better suited
to lead the EV revolution.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:32.370
Millions of Chinese are entering the middle
class, looking to buy their first car, and

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.160
have no stigma against electric vehicles.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.910
It’s great for local companies, it helps
grow the larger economy, and it’s good for

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.520
everyone’s lungs.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:48.100
So it’s no surprise that China buys more
EVs than the rest of the world combined.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:52.130
In the U.S., there’s California and everywhere
else.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:58.090
The Golden State buys almost ten times more
EVs than second place New York.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:03.430
In Palo Alto, you turn your head when you
see a car that’s not a Tesla.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:06.960
China is to the world, what California is
to the U.S.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.840
It’s making EVs irresistible.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.970
Like police cars and ambulances, they’re
exempt from the last-number rule, and have

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:18.210
a separate line to apply for a license plate.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:23.610
Many don’t even require a driver’s license
at all, with a top speed of around 40 miles

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:24.680
an hour.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.490
Smart Cars cost 20-30,000 dollars.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.530
These cost about 1.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:35.490
The government has given Tesla permission
to build a factory outside Shanghai,

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.490
Converted all Shenzhen busses to electric,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.360
and doubled its charging infrastructure in
the last year.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.610
Of course, cars are only part of the problem.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:51.520
China also burns an insane amount of coal,
one of the worst environmental offenders.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.400
Even India pales in comparison.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.170
But these numbers are somewhat misleading.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:03.570
There are three common types of coal plants
- subcritical, supercritical and ultra-supercritical.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:09.750
The first being low-temperature and less efficient,
the last being high-temperature and more efficient.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:14.610
Over time, China has been building fewer and
fewer subcritical plants, and more and more

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.840
efficient ones.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:21.120
By 2020, it’s estimated that every Chinese
coal plant will be more efficient than every

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.270
coal plant in America.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:26.000
China doesn’t have nearly as much natural
gas.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.720
So, instead, it’s largely moving straight
to renewable energy, which it now produces

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.990
more of than any other country.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.320
Like electric cars, its government has an
economic incentive.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.970
Compared to fossil fuel, renewable energy
usually creates far more jobs.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:47.090
In fact, coal employs fewer Americans than
fast food chain Arby’s.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:51.460
Already, Beijing’s air pollution has fallen
by 35%.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:57.760
This, according to experts, could save 20
million residents over 3 years of their lives.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:04.120
And even when its weaker economy in 2018 made
car sales fall to a two-decade low, electric

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:06.920
and hybrid cars continued to grow.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:12.560
At a time when the U.S. is removing EV tax
credits and environmental protections, it’s

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.950
more important than ever that China take the
lead.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:19.870
Its electric car revolution is just getting
started.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:24.640
Its anyone’s guess how the economy will
change in the next 20 years, but it’s always

